1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:05,340
I build software on my own, no other developer, no tester.

2
00:00:05,980 --> 00:00:11,840
AI writes most of my code now, and the only thing telling me it's done is the AI that just wrote it.

3
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:21,360
Three jobs for one AI, a password page, a payment page, and one that imports a spreadsheet.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:26,220
Where it ends up, the second AI finds bugs but can't touch the code.

5
00:00:26,220 --> 00:00:28,380
A third that fixes them.

6
00:00:30,440 --> 00:00:33,580
A real ticket, the way I would write one.

7
00:00:33,820 --> 00:00:37,640
People forget their password, we reset it by hand for a week.

8
00:00:38,120 --> 00:00:41,880
Three steps, not a word about what could go wrong. That's normal.

9
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:49,280
Before any of that, I wrote 18 rules the code has to obey.

10
00:00:49,760 --> 00:00:53,260
Links expire, two payments at once, only one goes through.

11
00:00:53,260 --> 00:00:55,920
I call the last one the double charge.

12
00:00:59,300 --> 00:01:06,340
I never showed them to it. Hand an AI the marking scheme, and it does, and it just does the marking scheme.

13
00:01:06,760 --> 00:01:13,500
And that's not me being unfair. Somebody says, build me a login page, and every rule stays in their head.

14
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:22,660
One AI, one go, no review.

15
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:28,780
What most people are doing right now, three jobs under 30 seconds, about $0.08 each.

16
00:01:29,220 --> 00:01:34,740
It did everything the ticket asked. 11 of the 18 rules passed, 7 are broken.

17
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:44,780
All three you just read, including the double charge, two payments land together, both go through.

18
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:47,220
And four more underneath.

19
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:03,320
So I added a second AI, I call it the checker, and its only job is to attack that code.

20
00:02:03,320 --> 00:02:07,100
It can't touch anything, all it can do is write a list.

21
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:15,900
The list goes to a third AI, that's the fixer, and that one changes the code.

22
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:20,660
Finding the problem and fixing it are two jobs, so there are two AIs.

23
00:02:24,860 --> 00:02:28,300
What comes back isn't a tick, it's this.

24
00:02:28,300 --> 00:02:35,980
Four problems raised, five still open after the repair, it went up, knocked down, and it says so.

25
00:02:36,540 --> 00:02:42,800
And the top line, this is a recommendation, not an approval. It doesn't decide, I do.

26
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:58,400
Same three jobs both ways, same model writing the code, a script scores both, and it can't see which version it's grading.

27
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:08,720
One AI passes 11, three AIs pass 14, four runs of the normal one, three of mine, 11 every time on the left.

28
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:15,180
That's the change, a reviewer isn't allowed to edit a repair step, and a second look.

29
00:03:19,360 --> 00:03:26,500
This is the bit I nearly missed, 14, three times, but it isn't the same 14.

30
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:30,480
Two runs, fix four, and break one.

31
00:03:30,480 --> 00:03:38,680
The third, fixes three, breaks nothing. So I ran the same pipeline three more times, 12 and 15.

32
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:44,780
It doesn't reliably fix four, it reliably fixes two, and the total was luck.

33
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:52,600
The only reason I found that is I'd written every run down as I went.

34
00:03:53,140 --> 00:03:58,940
Every experiment I ran is in there. The last one is me pulling a step out to see if I was doing anything.

35
00:03:58,940 --> 00:04:00,500
It wasn't.

36
00:04:04,800 --> 00:04:11,600
Sometimes the checker is certain that a problem that isn't there and the fixer believes it, so prove it first.

37
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:19,720
Four becomes five, six, and eight. Same setup, three runs, it stopped being a number I could trust.

38
00:04:20,200 --> 00:04:25,840
It blocked two findings that were right, and it hadn't stopped the wrong one either. One and three both ways.

39
00:04:31,960 --> 00:04:36,160
Here's what I got wrong. The gate was grading how well the checker argued.

40
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:43,200
That's the one thing AI is brilliant at faking, and the finding that hurt me was wrong in a completely different way.

41
00:04:43,580 --> 00:04:47,000
Acting on it broke this rule, and breaking this rule is a double charge.

42
00:04:49,760 --> 00:04:54,020
So don't create the argument after every repair, look at the code again.

43
00:04:54,020 --> 00:05:01,060
That version passes 14 up from 11. Run the scoring six times, and it lands between 12 and 15.

44
00:05:01,620 --> 00:05:08,340
Every one of them is above 11. It still breaks one and two runs out of three.

45
00:05:08,720 --> 00:05:11,560
Three jobs isn't many. That's the first thing I change.
